{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1844 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "df79af7b2a63d5695ef0ebe021f463aba7bf0964511f401e1bc3f3f6684a4735" :edb/db-before-t 1843 :edb/db-after-t 1844 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535156 50 #inst "2026-10-08T18:13:19.672Z" 13194139535156 true] [17592186157387 1020 #edb/ref 17592186334322 13194139535156 true] [17592186157387 1020 #edb/ref 17592186334341 13194139535156 true] [17592186157387 1020 #edb/ref 17592186334394 13194139535156 true] [17592186157388 1020 #edb/ref 17592186334358 13194139535156 true] [17592186157388 1020 #edb/ref 17592186334377 13194139535156 true] [17592186334322 1017 #edb/ref 17592186334323 13194139535156 true] [17592186334322 1017 #edb/ref 17592186334329 13194139535156 true] [17592186334322 1017 #edb/ref 17592186334335 13194139535156 true] [17592186334322 1089 "q-337375" 13194139535156 true] [17592186334322 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535156 true] [17592186334322 1100 "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+4y=7.\n$$\n\nWe wish to solve this equation using the method of variation of parameters (VOP).\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n$\\,$ According to VOP, we assume a particular solution of the form {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Solving for $u,$ we have $u(x)=$ {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the particular solution is ${y}_{p}(x)=$ {{field-3}}." 13194139535156 true] [17592186334322 1113 #uuid "2c874b63-ba61-5224-951d-5ad10fbf990c" 13194139535156 true] [17592186334322 1145 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+4{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-4x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{-4x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude.\n\nTo find $u,$ we substitute our ansatz into the equation, apply the product rule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate ${u}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u{e}^{-4x})}^{′}+4(u{e}^{-4x}) & =7 \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-4x}-4u{e}^{-4x}+4u{e}^{-4x} & =7 \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-4x}-\\cancel{4u{e}^{-4x}}+\\cancel{4u{e}^{-4x}} & =7 \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-4x} & =7 \\\\ {u}^{′} & =7{e}^{4x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & =7{e}^{4x} \\\\ \\int {u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 7{e}^{4x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ u & =\\frac{7}{4}{e}^{4x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${y}_{p}=u(x){e}^{-4x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =u(x){e}^{-4x} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{4}{e}^{4x}\\cdot {e}^{-4x} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535156 true] [17592186334323 1000 #edb/ref 17592186334324 13194139535156 true] [17592186334323 1000 #edb/ref 17592186334325 13194139535156 true] [17592186334323 1000 #edb/ref 17592186334326 13194139535156 true] [17592186334323 1000 #edb/ref 17592186334327 13194139535156 true] [17592186334323 1000 #edb/ref 17592186334328 13194139535156 true] [17592186334323 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045451 13194139535156 true] [17592186334323 1041 "field-1" 13194139535156 true] [17592186334323 1104 #edb/ref 17592186334328 13194139535156 true] [17592186334323 1110 #uuid "3473014a-7899-56f9-8c7f-3df389b43121" 13194139535156 true] [17592186334324 1034 "u(x){e}^{4x}" 13194139535156 true] [17592186334324 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535156 true] [17592186334324 1111 #uuid "64c67d11-b747-58f1-9c45-0d2dc1ee9620" 13194139535156 true] [17592186334325 1034 "u(x){e}^{-3x}" 13194139