{3x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{3x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude.\n\nTo find $u,$ we substitute our ansatz into the equation, apply the product rule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate ${u}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u{e}^{3x})}^{′}-3(u{e}^{3x}) & =6\\operatorname{cosh}⁡3x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{3x}+3u{e}^{3x}-3u{e}^{3x} & =6\\operatorname{cosh}⁡3x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{3x}+\\cancel{3u{e}^{3x}}-\\cancel{3u{e}^{3x}} & =6\\operatorname{cosh}⁡3x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{3x} & =6\\operatorname{cosh}⁡3x \\\\ {u}^{′} & =6{e}^{-3x}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡3x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cosh}⁡3x=\\frac{{e}^{3x}+{e}^{-3x}}{2}\n$$\n\nour equation for $u$ becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & =6{e}^{-3x}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡3x \\\\  & =6{e}^{-3x}(\\frac{{e}^{3x}+{e}^{-3x}}{2}) \\\\  & =3{e}^{-3x}({e}^{3x}+{e}^{-3x}) \\\\  & =3+3{e}^{-6x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & =3+3{e}^{-6x} \\\\ \\int {u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 3+3{e}^{-6x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ u & =3x-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-6x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${y}_{p}=u(x){e}^{3x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =u(x){e}^{3x} \\\\  & =3x{e}^{3x}-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535156 true] [17592186334378 1000 #edb/ref 17592186334379 13194139535156 true] [17592186334378 1000 #edb/ref 17592186334380 13194139535156 true] [17592186334378 1000 #edb/ref 17592186334381 13194139535156 true] [17592186334378 1000 #edb/ref 17592186334382 13194139535156 true] [17592186334378 1000 #edb/ref 17592186334383 1319